C={x["paper_id"]:x for x in json.loads((D/"interspeech-2025-thirtysixth-d3-papers.json").read_text())["papers"]};P={x["paper_id"]:x for x in json.loads((D/"interspeech-2025-papers.json").read_text())["papers"]}
N={
"villani25_interspeech":("A listener in noise may need speech made clearer after capture, but enhancement should not erase speech or make the result sound unnatural.","Noise statistics change over time and a useful near-end method has to estimate what belongs to the noise without relying on a clean reference.","Use one fixed noise estimate or raise all frequencies equally and assume intelligibility will follow.","Estimate long-term fractile noise statistics and use them to guide near-end listening enhancement, then test the method against speech and noise conditions.","The paper analyzes and extends a near-end listening-enhancement method based on long-term fractile noise statistics.","The method treats noise as a distribution over time rather than a single fixed level; enhancement is constrained by the estimated noise floor so speech structure is not indiscriminately removed.","The paper reports listening-enhancement results for the proposed statistical method and its extension.","Noise type, recordings, listeners, parameter settings, and perceptual tests limit generalization; improved quality is not identical to improved intelligibility."),
"vlasenko25_interspeech":("Sentence mode—such as a question or statement—is carried not only by words but also by prosody, and a useful model should work across languages and modalities.","Prosody varies with language, speaker, emotion, and recording; text alone misses timing and pitch while audio alone can miss lexical structure.","Predict sentence mode from text or one acoustic feature and assume the same cue works across languages.","Combine multimodal prosody representations and evaluate multilingual sentence-mode prediction under the actual interaction between words, voice, and language.","The study presents multimodal prosody modeling for multilingual sentence-mode prediction.","Sentence mode is a joint signal: lexical content provides one constraint while pitch, timing, and energy provide another, and disagreement between them is informative rather than noise.","The paper reports multilingual multimodal prosody-modeling results for sentence-mode prediction.","Languages, labels, speaker balance, modality quality, and task definition bound the claim; sentence mode is not a complete model of intent."),
"wagner25_interspeech":("A recognizer for dysarthric speech should adapt to a particular speaker even when only limited recordings are available.","Dysarthria changes the relation between intended words and acoustic realization; synthetic data can add coverage but can also repeat the wrong errors or sound unlike the speaker.","Fine-tune on a small personal set only, or add generic synthetic speech without controlling what variation it contributes.","Use LLM-generated transcripts to create controllable synthetic speech and personalize fine-tuning for the target speaker, then measure recognition under speaker-specific conditions.","The paper studies personalized fine-tuning with controllable synthetic speech for dysarthric speech recognition.","Personalization is a data-design problem: synthetic examples are useful only when their transcript, pronunciation variation, and speaker control support the target speaker rather than dilute them.","The paper reports recognition results for personalized adaptation using controllable synthetic data.","Speaker cohort, dysarthria type, transcript generation, synthesis quality, and adaptation budget limit generalization or clinical claims."),
"ong25_interspeech":("A low-resource language needs an honest speech-recognition benchmark before claims about progress can be compared.","Small corpora make train/test estimates unstable, spelling and dialect choices affect labels, and a benchmark can expose gaps without solving data scarcity.","Report one model score on a tiny corpus and treat it as a general language capability estimate.","Build the Faetar speech-recognition benchmark with documented recordings, splits, transcripts, and evaluation so later systems can be compared on the same problem.","The paper introduces the Faetar Speech Recognition Benchmark.","Benchmark construction is part of scientific knowledge: the dataset fixes what counts as an error and makes future improvements distinguishable from changes in collection or split.","The paper reports the benchmark resources and baseline recognition results for Faetar.","Corpus size, speakers, dialect coverage, transcription conventions, and split design bound conclusions about the wider language community."),
"ozer25_interspeech":("An audio watermark should survive ordinary processing and neural codecs while remaining detectable, otherwise it cannot support provenance or accountability in real systems.","Compression and generative codecs intentionally alter waveform details; a watermark can be detectable in clean audio yet disappear after the transformations people actually use.","Test only clean files or assume a watermark that survives one codec survives every neural codec.","Evaluate audio-watermarking algorithms under a broad real-world transformation suite, including neural codecs, and compare detectability and audio quality.","The paper provides a comprehensive real-world assessment of audio watermarking algorithms and asks whether they survive neural codecs.","Robustness is a chain of transformations: a watermark claim is meaningful only over an explicit threat/process set, with detectability balanced against audible distortion.","The paper reports comparative survival and failure patterns across watermarking methods and neural codecs.","Algorithms, codec versions, payloads, thresholds, and attack suite bound the result; survival in tested codecs is not universal tamper resistance."),
"okamoto25_interspeech":("A compact system should translate simultaneous speech and produce multiple sound spots on a laptop carried in a backpack, without assuming a server or a quiet turn-taking environment.","Simultaneous speech translation combines overlap, latency, translation uncertainty, and spatial/audio output under tight compute and mobility constraints.","Wait for a complete utterance, send it to a large remote system, or treat translation and sound placement as separate problems.","Integrate simultaneous speech translation with compact multiple-sound-spot synthesis in a portable system and evaluate the complete interaction loop.","The paper presents a simultaneous speech-translation system integrated with compact multiple sound-spot synthesis on a laptop carried in a backpack.","The system boundary is the product: recognition, translation, timing, and spatialized output must jointly meet latency and resource constraints rather than optimize isolated modules.","The paper reports an integrated portable-system demonstration and evaluation.","Language pair, overlap conditions, hardware, latency measurement, and user setting limit generalization to broad simultaneous conversation."),
"oconnorrussell25_interspeech":("People use gaze and other visible cues to signal that a turn is ending or beginning, especially when noise makes audio timing unreliable.","Turn-taking is a coupled prediction-and-action problem: visual cues can arrive before words finish, while noise corrupts the acoustic evidence and social norms vary.","Predict the next turn from audio alone or treat a detected pause as a universal handoff signal.","Add visual cues to turn-taking prediction and test whether they make decisions more robust in noise.","The study evaluates visual cues for robust turn-taking prediction in noise.","A turn is a coordination event between people, not merely a boundary in a waveform; combining channels lets one cue compensate when another is degraded.","The paper reports that visual cues support more robust turn-taking prediction under noisy conditions.","Participants, camera viewpoint, interaction task, noise type, timing labels, and model latency bound the claim; a lab cue is not a universal conversational rule."),
"lu25c_interspeech":("A speech-aware language model should follow spoken instructions and retain earlier abilities after learning new ones.","Instruction following can improve while unrelated skills are forgotten; speech adds recognition errors and modality-specific variation to the usual language-model tradeoff.","Measure only new-task accuracy or train on new instruction data and assume old abilities remain intact.","Use Speech-IFEval to test instruction-following behavior and quantify catastrophic forgetting across speech-aware tasks.","The paper introduces Speech-IFEval and evaluates instruction-following and forgetting in speech-aware language models.","Evaluation must pair compliance with retention: a model that follows a new command by losing old behavior has shifted capability rather than simply improved it.","The paper reports benchmark results for spoken instruction following and catastrophic forgetting.","Task suite, speech recognition quality, prompts, model families, and training order bound the conclusions; benchmark retention is not proof of reliable deployment behavior."),
}
